---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const emailSubject = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=Spark%20Ads%20%E2%80%94%20tell%20us%20about%20your%20brand";
const calHref = "https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first";
---

<Layout
  title="Spark Ads — The Complete Guide to TikTok's Native Ad Format | OKAD"
  description="TikTok Spark Ads let brands boost organic posts as paid ads while retaining all engagement. This guide covers how they work, authorization codes, budgeting, optimization, and how to combine them with Canvas UGC for maximum ROI."
>
  <Header />
  <main>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="pt-20 md:pt-24 pb-10 text-center bg-bg">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium text-accent bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.18)] rounded-full px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-6">
          Open reference &middot; September 2026
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(28px,5vw,48px)] leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink mb-4">
          Spark Ads: <span class="text-accent">The Complete Guide</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-[18px] text-ink-2 max-w-[600px] mx-auto leading-[1.6] mb-8">
          TikTok Spark Ads let brands boost existing organic posts as paid ads &mdash; keeping all likes, comments, and shares intact. This guide covers how they work, how to set them up, and how to combine them with Canvas UGC for maximum ROI.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3 justify-center flex-wrap mb-8">
          <a href={emailSubject} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent-hover transition-all no-underline">Order Spark Ads setup &rarr;</a>
          <a href={calHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-card text-ink border border-border hover:border-accent hover:text-accent transition-all no-underline">Book a call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-5 flex-wrap justify-center font-mono text-[12px] text-muted">
          <span>TikTok</span>
          <span>+142% engagement</span>
          <span>Native format</span>
          <span>Organic &rarr; paid</span>
          <span>From $50/day</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How it works grid -->
    <section class="py-8">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-display text-[22px] font-bold tracking-[-0.02em] text-center mb-5">How Spark Ads Work</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3.5">
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F3AC;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Post Goes Organic</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">A brand or creator publishes a native TikTok post. It collects organic views, likes, comments, and shares.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F3AF;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Authorize &amp; Boost</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">The post is authorized for paid promotion via TikTok Ads Manager. All existing engagement carries over.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4B0;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Scale the Winner</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Paid reach amplifies a proven organic post. New engagement merges back into the original &mdash; compounding social proof.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <section class="py-10 border-t border-b border-border">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-muted-2 mb-4">Contents</div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
          {[
            { num: "01", text: "Overview", href: "#overview" },
            { num: "02", text: "How Spark Ads Work", href: "#mechanics" },
            { num: "03", text: "Two Types of Spark Ads", href: "#types" },
            { num: "04", text: "Spark Ads vs Standard In-Feed Ads", href: "#comparison" },
            { num: "05", text: "Canvas UGC + Spark Ads", href: "#canvas-ugc" },
            { num: "06", text: "How to Identify Winners", href: "#winners" },
            { num: "07", text: "Budget and Bidding", href: "#budget" },
            { num: "08", text: "Key Metrics and Optimization", href: "#metrics" },
            { num: "09", text: "Glossary", href: "#glossary" },
            { num: "10", text: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
          ].map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-[14px] text-ink-2 font-medium hover:bg-bg-alt transition-colors no-underline">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium text-accent min-w-[22px]">{item.num}</span>
              {item.text}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01 Overview -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="overview">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">01 &mdash; Overview</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Organic posts, paid reach, compounding engagement</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Spark Ads boost existing organic TikTok posts as paid ads</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">All organic engagement (likes, comments, shares) is retained</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">142% higher engagement, 25% higher CTR, 24% higher CVR than standard ads</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Spark Ads are TikTok's native advertising format that lets brands promote existing organic posts &mdash; either from the brand's own account or from a creator's account &mdash; as paid ads. Unlike standard in-feed ads, which are created from scratch inside TikTok Ads Manager, Spark Ads use real posts that already live on TikTok. The ad links back to the original post, and all engagement generated by paid reach merges into the organic post's metrics.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This distinction matters because social proof compounds. When a user sees a Spark Ad with 15,000 likes and 400 comments, that credibility came from organic engagement &mdash; not from ad spend. New viewers interact with the post naturally, and their likes and comments add to the total. The result is a paid ad that looks and feels like organic content, because it is organic content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">TikTok's own data shows that Spark Ads deliver 142% higher engagement rates than non-Spark ads, 25% higher click-through rates than standard in-feed ads, and 24% higher conversion rates than ads run from a brand's own account without the Spark format. These numbers reflect a simple reality: users engage more with content that feels native to their feed.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Spark Ads are especially powerful when combined with a high-volume organic content strategy like <a href="/canvas-ugc" class="text-accent hover:underline">Canvas UGC</a>. Post 40+ organic videos per month, identify the top performers, and promote them with paid budget. The organic phase serves as a free testing ground; the paid phase scales what already works.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 How Spark Ads Work -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="mechanics">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">02 &mdash; Mechanics</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How Spark Ads work under the hood</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Brand authorizes an organic post for paid promotion in TikTok Ads Manager</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Paid views and engagement merge into the original post</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The ad links to the creator's or brand's real TikTok profile</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">At a technical level, Spark Ads work by linking an existing TikTok post to a campaign inside TikTok Ads Manager. The ad unit is not a separate piece of creative &mdash; it is the original post, served to a targeted paid audience. When someone taps the avatar or username on a Spark Ad, they land on the actual TikTok profile that published the post, not a disconnected landing page.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This is fundamentally different from standard in-feed ads, where the creative exists only inside Ads Manager and engagement disappears when the campaign ends. With Spark Ads, every like, comment, share, and follow generated during the paid campaign is permanently attributed to the original organic post. The post continues to accumulate engagement even after the ad budget is exhausted.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The setup process</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Publish an organic post.</strong> The post goes live on TikTok and begins collecting organic engagement. Let it run for 24&ndash;72 hours to gather initial performance signals.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Authorize the post.</strong> If it is the brand's own post, authorize it directly in TikTok Ads Manager. If it is a creator's post, the creator generates an authorization code from their TikTok account settings.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Create the Spark Ad campaign.</strong> In TikTok Ads Manager, create a new campaign, select "Spark Ads" as the ad format, and paste the authorization code or select the brand's own post.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Set targeting and budget.</strong> Define your audience targeting, daily budget, bid strategy, and campaign objective (traffic, conversions, app installs, etc.).</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Launch and monitor.</strong> The ad goes live. Paid engagement merges into the original post. Monitor CTR, CVR, CPA, and engagement rate to optimize.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4 mt-6">One important nuance: Spark Ads support multiple campaign objectives &mdash; including traffic, conversions, app installs, video views, and reach. The choice of objective determines how TikTok's algorithm optimizes delivery. For most performance-focused brands, conversion or traffic objectives paired with Spark Ads produce the strongest results, because the native format reduces friction while the algorithm optimizes for the desired action.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 Two Types of Spark Ads -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="types">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">03 &mdash; Types</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Two types of Spark Ads</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Type 1: Boost the brand's own TikTok posts</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Type 2: Boost a creator's post via authorization code</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Authorization codes are valid for 7, 30, or 60 days</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Type 1: Brand's own posts</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The simplest form. The brand publishes a post on its own TikTok account, then promotes it as a Spark Ad directly through TikTok Ads Manager. No authorization code is needed &mdash; the brand already controls the account. This is the default path for brands running <a href="/canvas-ugc" class="text-accent hover:underline">Canvas UGC</a> programs, where creators post on brand-owned accounts. Every post on the brand's canvas is immediately eligible for Spark Ads promotion.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The advantage of promoting brand-owned posts is operational simplicity. There is no code exchange, no expiration window, and no risk of a creator revoking access mid-campaign. The brand has full control over the creative lifecycle &mdash; from organic publication to paid amplification to eventual archival.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Type 2: Creator's posts via authorization code</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When a brand wants to promote a post that lives on a creator's personal TikTok account, the creator must generate an authorization code. This is done inside TikTok's app under Settings &rarr; Creator Tools &rarr; Ad Authorization. The creator selects the specific post, chooses a validity period (7, 30, or 60 days), and generates a code. The brand then inputs this code in TikTok Ads Manager to link the post to their ad campaign.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This approach is common in influencer and <a href="/ugc-whitelisting" class="text-accent hover:underline">whitelisting</a> strategies, where the brand benefits from the creator's authentic voice and audience. The ad appears under the creator's handle, which adds credibility. However, it requires coordination: the creator must grant access proactively, the code has an expiration date, and the creator can revoke authorization at any time.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Authorization code validity</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Validity period</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Best for</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">7 days</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Quick tests, one-off promotions</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Minimal commitment from creator; renew if the ad performs well</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">30 days</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Standard campaigns, ongoing partnerships</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Most common choice; balances flexibility with campaign duration</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">60 days</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Long-running evergreen campaigns</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Best for proven performers you want to scale over time</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A practical tip: always request 30-day authorization as the default. Seven days is too short for meaningful optimization, and 60 days is only worth it for posts you have already validated. If you are working with creators through an agency like OKAD, the authorization code exchange is handled as part of the production workflow &mdash; creators know the process and provide codes promptly.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 Spark Ads vs Standard In-Feed Ads -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="comparison">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">04 &mdash; Comparison</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Spark Ads vs standard in-feed ads</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Spark Ads retain engagement; standard ads do not</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Spark Ads link to a real profile; standard ads link to a landing page</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Spark Ads outperform on every major metric</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The core difference is ownership. A standard in-feed ad is a piece of creative that exists only inside TikTok Ads Manager. It has no organic presence, no profile link, and no persistent engagement. When the campaign ends, the ad disappears. A Spark Ad, by contrast, is a real post that lives on a real profile. It accumulates engagement over time, and that engagement persists whether the ad is running or not.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This distinction drives measurable performance differences across every metric that matters.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Dimension</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Spark Ads</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Standard in-feed ads</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Engagement rate</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">+142% vs non-Spark</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Baseline</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Click-through rate (CTR)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">+25% vs standard</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Baseline</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Conversion rate (CVR)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">+24% vs brand-account ads</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Baseline</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Social proof</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Retains and compounds organic engagement</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Starts at zero; lost when campaign ends</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Profile link</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Links to real TikTok profile</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Links to landing page or app store</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Native feel</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Indistinguishable from organic in-feed content</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Clearly marked as ad; feels interruptive</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Post-campaign value</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Post continues earning organic reach</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">No residual value</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Creative source</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Existing organic post</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Custom creative uploaded to Ads Manager</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The performance advantage of Spark Ads comes from a single factor: users do not perceive them as ads. A Spark Ad appears in the For You feed with a real username, real comments, and real like counts. It behaves exactly like an organic post &mdash; because it is one. Standard in-feed ads, by contrast, are clearly marked, link to external destinations, and lack the social proof that drives trust-based engagement.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For brands running <a href="/ugc-ads" class="text-accent hover:underline">UGC ad campaigns</a>, Spark Ads are almost always the better format. The organic foundation provides a performance floor that standard ads cannot match. The only scenario where standard in-feed ads make more sense is when the brand needs to run creative that does not exist as an organic post &mdash; for example, a highly produced commercial or a creative with a specific CTA overlay that would not work as organic content.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 Canvas UGC + Spark Ads -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="canvas-ugc">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">05 &mdash; Canvas UGC + Spark Ads</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The organic-to-paid pipeline</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Post 40+ organic videos per month on brand accounts</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Identify top 5&ndash;10% performers based on organic signals</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Promote winners as Spark Ads with paid budget</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The most powerful application of Spark Ads is not as a standalone tactic &mdash; it is as the paid amplification layer on top of a high-volume organic content strategy. This is where <a href="/canvas-ugc" class="text-accent hover:underline">Canvas UGC</a> and Spark Ads become a unified system.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The logic is straightforward. In a Canvas UGC program, a team of 10+ creators produces 40 or more organic videos per month on brand-owned TikTok accounts. Most of these videos will perform at baseline levels. But a small percentage &mdash; typically 5&ndash;10% &mdash; will break out with above-average retention, engagement, and share rates. These are the winners. Instead of guessing which creative will work as a paid ad (the traditional approach), you let TikTok's organic algorithm identify winners for free. Then you put paid budget behind proven content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This approach eliminates the most expensive problem in paid social advertising: creative testing. Traditional creative testing requires spending $50&ndash;$200 per ad variant just to gather enough data to judge performance. With Canvas UGC + Spark Ads, the organic phase is the test. By the time you spend a single dollar on paid promotion, you already know the content works.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The pipeline in practice</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Produce at volume.</strong> 10+ creators post 3&ndash;5 videos each per week on brand accounts. Target 40+ posts per month across all accounts.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Monitor organic performance.</strong> Track retention rate, engagement rate, saves, shares, and comments for every post over 48&ndash;72 hours.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Flag top performers.</strong> Any post that exceeds 2x the account's average engagement rate or retention rate is a Spark Ad candidate.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Launch Spark Ad campaigns.</strong> Promote flagged posts with $50&ndash;$100/day initial budget. Test multiple campaign objectives (traffic, conversions) per post.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Scale or kill.</strong> After 3&ndash;5 days of paid data, double budget on winners and pause underperformers. Reallocate spend weekly.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Feat-card: OKAD Canvas UGC + Spark Ads -->
        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-6 my-6 flex gap-4 items-start flex-col sm:flex-row">
