    <!-- 06 How to Identify Winners -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="winners">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; Identifying Winners</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How to pick the right posts to Spark</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Retention rate is the single most important signal</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Saves and shares indicate intent, not just passive viewing</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Wait 48&ndash;72 hours for organic signals before promoting</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Not every organic post deserves paid budget. The goal is to identify the small percentage of posts that have already proven their ability to hold attention and drive engagement organically. Promoting a mediocre post with paid spend does not make it better &mdash; it just makes it a mediocre post with more impressions.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The selection process starts 48&ndash;72 hours after publication, once TikTok's algorithm has had time to distribute the post organically and gather meaningful performance data. Evaluating too early (under 24 hours) risks promoting a post that got a lucky initial push but lacks staying power. Waiting too long (over 5 days) means missing the window where paid amplification compounds the organic momentum.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Key signals to evaluate</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Signal</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">What it measures</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Spark threshold</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Retention rate</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">% of viewers who watch past key moments (3s, 50%, full)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Above 50% at 3 seconds; above 30% at halfway</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Engagement rate</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">(Likes + comments + shares) / views</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">2x+ the account's 30-day average</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Save rate</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Saves / views &mdash; indicates high-intent interest</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Above 2% (strong signal of utility or desire)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Share rate</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Shares / views &mdash; indicates content worth spreading</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Above 1% (strong virality indicator)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Comment quality</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Whether comments show genuine interest, questions, tags</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Presence of "where do I get this?" or friend-tagging</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">View velocity</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">How quickly the post accumulates views in first 48h</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Top 10% of the account's recent posts by 48h view count</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Retention rate is the most reliable predictor of paid performance. A post with high retention tells TikTok's algorithm that viewers find the content worth watching, which translates directly into lower CPMs and better delivery when the post is promoted as a Spark Ad. Save rate is the second-most important signal, because saves indicate intent &mdash; the viewer found something useful enough to return to later.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Avoid the trap of promoting posts purely based on view count. A post with 100,000 views but low retention and low engagement often performs poorly as a Spark Ad, because the algorithm already distributed it widely and the audience signal is diluted. A post with 15,000 views but exceptional retention and save rates is almost always the better Spark Ad candidate.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 Budget and Bidding -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="budget">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Budget &amp; Bidding</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Budget, bidding, and scaling strategy</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Start at $50&ndash;$100/day per Spark Ad test</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Scale winners by 20&ndash;30% per day to avoid resetting the algorithm</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Kill underperformers after 3&ndash;5 days of data</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Spark Ads budgeting follows the same principles as any TikTok paid campaign, with one important difference: because the creative has already been validated organically, the risk of wasting budget on bad creative is substantially lower. This means you can start with lower test budgets and scale faster once performance is confirmed.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Starting budget</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For most brands, $50&ndash;$100 per day per Spark Ad is the right starting point. This budget is large enough to exit TikTok's learning phase (typically 50 conversions) within a reasonable timeframe, but small enough that you can test 3&ndash;5 Spark Ads simultaneously without overcommitting. A monthly Spark Ads testing budget of $3,000&ndash;$5,000 is sufficient for most mid-market brands to run a continuous test-and-scale cycle.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Scaling strategy</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When a Spark Ad is performing well (CTR above target, CPA below threshold), increase the daily budget by 20&ndash;30% per day. Avoid doubling budgets overnight &mdash; TikTok's algorithm re-enters the learning phase when budgets change dramatically, which can temporarily degrade performance. Gradual scaling preserves the algorithm's optimization and maintains consistent delivery.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For high-performing Spark Ads, consider duplicating the ad group with a higher budget rather than scaling the original. This allows the original ad group to continue performing at its optimized level while the duplicate explores a larger audience at the new budget. Compare results over 48 hours and keep the better performer.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Bid strategies</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Strategy</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">When to use</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Risk level</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Lowest cost (auto bid)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Initial testing phase; let TikTok find the cheapest conversions</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Low &mdash; safe default for new campaigns</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Cost cap</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Scaling phase; set a target CPA and let the algorithm optimize within it</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Medium &mdash; may under-deliver if cap is too aggressive</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Bid cap</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Mature campaigns with clear CPA targets and high volume</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Higher &mdash; requires accurate CPA data to set correctly</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Start with lowest cost bidding for every new Spark Ad. Switch to cost cap once you have 50+ conversions and a clear understanding of your target CPA. Bid cap is reserved for mature campaigns where you have enough historical data to set precise bid limits without throttling delivery.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">One often-overlooked advantage of Spark Ads: because the organic post continues to earn free organic reach alongside the paid campaign, the effective cost per result is lower than what TikTok Ads Manager reports. The platform only counts paid impressions in its CPA calculation, but the organic impressions that compound during the campaign period are a free bonus that reduces true blended cost.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Key Metrics and Optimization -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="metrics">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Metrics &amp; Optimization</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Key metrics and how to optimize them</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">CTR, CVR, CPA, and engagement rate are the four metrics that matter</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Optimize creative first, targeting second, bidding third</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Refresh creative every 7&ndash;14 days to prevent fatigue</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Spark Ads optimization follows the same hierarchy as any paid social campaign: creative quality drives the majority of performance, followed by audience targeting, then bid strategy. The difference with Spark Ads is that creative quality has already been validated by organic performance, so the optimization focus shifts more heavily toward targeting and scaling decisions.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The four metrics that matter</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Metric</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">What it tells you</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Benchmark</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">CTR (click-through rate)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">How effectively the ad drives clicks to your destination</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">1&ndash;3% for Spark Ads (vs 0.5&ndash;1.5% for standard)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">CVR (conversion rate)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">% of clickers who complete the desired action</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Varies by vertical; track against your baseline</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">CPA (cost per action)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Total spend divided by conversions</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Should be 15&ndash;30% lower than standard ad CPA</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink font-medium">Engagement rate</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">Total paid + organic interactions / impressions</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-ink-2">3&ndash;8% for well-performing Spark Ads</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Optimization playbook</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Days 1&ndash;3: Observe.</strong> Let the campaign run without changes. TikTok needs time to exit the learning phase. Do not adjust budgets, targeting, or bids during this window.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Days 3&ndash;5: Evaluate.</strong> Check CTR, CVR, and CPA against your targets. If CTR is below 1%, the creative may not resonate with the paid audience &mdash; test a different Spark Ad. If CTR is strong but CVR is low, the issue is likely the landing page or offer, not the ad.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Days 5&ndash;7: Optimize.</strong> Scale winners by 20&ndash;30% daily budget increase. Pause underperformers. Test new audience segments on proven creative. Avoid changing creative and targeting simultaneously.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Days 7&ndash;14: Refresh.</strong> Even high-performing Spark Ads experience creative fatigue. Monitor frequency &mdash; when average frequency exceeds 3&ndash;4, introduce new organic winners from your content pipeline.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Ongoing: Maintain the pipeline.</strong> The organic-to-paid pipeline must be continuous. New organic content every week feeds new Spark Ad candidates. A steady supply of fresh creative prevents fatigue from ever becoming a bottleneck.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Feat-card: OKAD optimization -->
        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-6 my-6 flex gap-4 items-start flex-col sm:flex-row">
          <div class="w-11 h-11 rounded-[10px] bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.12)] flex items-center justify-center text-[20px] shrink-0">&#9889;</div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-[16px] font-semibold mb-1"><a href={emailSubject} class="text-ink hover:text-accent transition-colors no-underline">OKAD handles Spark Ads optimization end-to-end</a></h4>
            <p class="text-[14px] text-muted m-0 leading-[1.5]">Our team monitors performance daily, manages budget scaling, refreshes creative from the organic pipeline, and reports weekly on CTR, CVR, CPA, and ROAS. You focus on your product &mdash; we handle the ads.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">One critical point about Spark Ads optimization: the biggest lever is always the creative. Targeting and bidding adjustments produce incremental improvements, but the difference between a great Spark Ad and a mediocre one is 3&ndash;5x in performance. This is why the continuous organic content pipeline is essential &mdash; it ensures you always have fresh, validated creative to rotate into paid campaigns. For more on how to structure creative testing across your entire ad account, see our <a href="/guide" class="text-accent hover:underline">creative testing guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Glossary -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="glossary">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Glossary</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Glossary</h2>
        {[
          { term: "Spark Ads", def: "TikTok's native ad format that lets brands promote existing organic posts as paid ads. All engagement is retained on the original post." },
          { term: "Authorization code", def: "A code generated by a TikTok creator that grants a brand permission to use the creator's post as a Spark Ad. Valid for 7, 30, or 60 days." },
          { term: "In-feed ad", def: "A standard TikTok ad that appears in the For You feed. Created inside Ads Manager with no organic presence." },
          { term: "CTR (click-through rate)", def: "The percentage of ad viewers who click through to the destination URL or landing page. Calculated as clicks / impressions." },
          { term: "CVR (conversion rate)", def: "The percentage of ad clickers who complete the desired action (purchase, sign-up, install). Calculated as conversions / clicks." },
          { term: "CPA (cost per action)", def: "The average cost to acquire one conversion. Calculated as total ad spend / total conversions." },
          { term: "CPM (cost per mille)", def: "The cost per 1,000 impressions. Used to compare relative cost efficiency across campaigns and platforms." },
          { term: "ROAS (return on ad spend)", def: "Revenue generated divided by ad spend. A ROAS of 3.0 means $3 in revenue for every $1 spent." },
          { term: "Learning phase", def: "The initial period (typically 50 conversions) during which TikTok's algorithm optimizes ad delivery. Performance may be volatile during this phase." },
          { term: "Creative fatigue", def: "The decline in ad performance that occurs when the same audience sees the same creative too many times. Monitored via frequency metrics." },
          { term: "Retention rate", def: "The percentage of viewers who continue watching a video past key timestamps (3 seconds, midpoint, completion). The strongest organic signal for Spark Ad selection." },
          { term: "Canvas UGC", def: "A content delivery model where creators post on brand-owned accounts at volume. See the full guide at /canvas-ugc." },
          { term: "Whitelisting", def: "Granting a brand permission to run paid ads through a creator's account or using a creator's content. See /ugc-whitelisting." },
          { term: "Partnership Ads", def: "Meta's equivalent of Spark Ads, where a brand promotes a creator's post as a paid ad on Facebook or Instagram. See /partnership-ads." },
          { term: "Branded Content Ads", def: "Instagram and TikTok's format for promoting creator posts that disclose a brand partnership. See /branded-content-ads." },
        ].map((item) => (
          <div class="py-4 border-b border-border">
            <div class="font-display font-semibold text-ink mb-1">{item.term}</div>
            <div class="text-[14px] text-muted">{item.def}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 FAQ -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="faq">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">10 &mdash; FAQ</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        {[
          { q: "What is the difference between Spark Ads and regular TikTok ads?", a: "Spark Ads promote existing organic TikTok posts as paid ads. Regular in-feed ads are created from scratch inside Ads Manager. The key difference is that Spark Ads retain all organic engagement (likes, comments, shares) and link to a real TikTok profile, while regular ads have no organic presence and disappear when the campaign ends." },
          { q: "Do I need a creator's permission to run a Spark Ad on their post?", a: "Yes. If the post is on a creator's account, the creator must generate an authorization code from TikTok's Creator Tools settings. The code grants you permission to use their post as a Spark Ad for 7, 30, or 60 days. If the post is on your own brand account, no authorization code is needed." },
          { q: "How much should I spend on Spark Ads?", a: "Start with $50-$100 per day per Spark Ad. This is enough to exit TikTok's learning phase and gather meaningful performance data. A monthly testing budget of $3,000-$5,000 allows you to run 3-5 simultaneous tests and continuously rotate in new creative. Scale winning ads by 20-30% per day." },
          { q: "How do I know which organic posts to promote as Spark Ads?", a: "Look for posts with above-average retention rates (50%+ at 3 seconds), engagement rates (2x your account average), and high save/share ratios. Wait 48-72 hours after publication for organic signals to stabilize before making a decision. Prioritize retention rate over raw view count." },
          { q: "Can I use Spark Ads with content from multiple creators?", a: "Yes. In fact, testing Spark Ads from multiple creators is recommended. Different creators resonate with different audience segments. Run 3-5 Spark Ads from different creators simultaneously, compare performance, and scale the winners. This is especially effective with a Canvas UGC program where you have 10+ creators producing content." },
          { q: "What happens to the engagement when the Spark Ad campaign ends?", a: "All engagement generated during the paid campaign stays on the original organic post permanently. Likes, comments, shares, and follows earned during the campaign are retained. The post also continues to earn organic reach after the campaign ends, often at a higher baseline than before because the algorithm sees the post as high-performing." },
          { q: "Can Spark Ads be used with other ad formats?", a: "Yes. A post that performs well as a Spark Ad on TikTok can also be repurposed for Partnership Ads on Meta, Branded Content Ads on Instagram, or standard UGC ad campaigns on other platforms. The organic-to-paid pipeline on TikTok is often the starting point for a multi-platform paid content strategy." },
          { q: "How often should I refresh my Spark Ads creative?", a: "Monitor frequency metrics. When average frequency exceeds 3-4 (meaning the average user has seen the ad 3-4 times), introduce new creative. For most campaigns, this means refreshing every 7-14 days. A continuous organic content pipeline ensures you always have fresh, validated creative ready to promote." },
          { q: "Can OKAD manage my Spark Ads?", a: "Yes. OKAD runs the full organic-to-paid pipeline: we produce 40+ organic videos per month through our creator network, identify top performers using retention and engagement data, set up and optimize Spark Ads campaigns, and provide weekly reporting on all key metrics. Email olga@okad.agency to discuss your brand's Spark Ads strategy." },
          { q: "Do Spark Ads work for e-commerce brands or only for apps?", a: "Spark Ads work across all verticals. E-commerce brands use them to promote product demos, unboxing videos, and customer testimonials. App companies use them for install campaigns. SaaS brands use them to promote tutorial and use-case content. The format is effective wherever authentic, native-feeling content drives conversions." },
        ].map((item) => (
